    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "All tests green.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec required all rejection responses (400, 409, 429) to return an RFC7807 `application/problem+json` body with `type`, `title`, `status`, and `detail`.\n\nAuditing the escalation handlers showed the 409 handlers (`CityAtCapacity`, `DuplicateOwnerEmail/Household/Telephone`) and the 429 handler (`DailyOwnerLimit`) already used `ProblemDetails.build(...)` returning a `ResponseEntity<ProblemDetail>` \u2014 which Spring MVC (invoked under the hood by the OfficeFloor starter's `SpringServerHttpConnection`) serializes as `application/problem+json`. Only three **400** handlers were non-conformant:\n\n- `FutureRegistrationDateExceptionHandler` \u2014 returned a plain `String` body\n- `InvalidPostcodeExceptionHandler` \u2014 returned a plain `String` body\n- `MissingOwnerFieldsExceptionHandler` \u2014 returned a `Map` (`{\"errors\":[...]}`) as `application/json`\n\nI converted each to the existing `ProblemDetails.build(ex, status, detail)` + `ResponseEntity<ProblemDetail>` pattern, keeping the HTTP status codes unchanged and preserving the missing-field list as an extra `errors` property on the problem body.\n\n**Keeping structural impact low:** I reused the existing `ProblemDetails` helper rather than adding logic, and touched only the three handlers that were actually non-conformant. Each handler is a single-method class (WMC_other = 0, CC = 1), so per-function cost stays minimal; I avoided growing any method or class and made no changes to the 409/429 handlers that were already compliant.\n\n`AcceptanceTest.coreRejectionIsProblemJson` (removes `city` \u2192 400) now sees `application/problem+json` with `$.status == 400`, and the full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`).",
